LAN-396 — the matching change to scripts/production/**, for Brian to apply.

Agents may not edit anything under `scripts/production/`, and this is where the
gap of 2026-09-17 actually lives. Until this diff is applied, a database built
from the baseline opens the 2026–27 season with **no onboarding item types**,
every membership generated for it has no onboarding items, Sub invoiced, Sub
paid, Comms group and Squad photo cannot be edited on the roster board, and a
membership record reads "This season has no onboarding items configured, so
this membership has none." That is exactly what production did, and what Brian
repaired by hand in the Supabase SQL editor the same day.

Apply it from the repository root:

    git apply docs/ux/review/LAN-396/production-scripts.diff

Nothing has to be re-run against the live database: the hand repair already
did what this file now does. The diff matters for the next database built from
the baseline, and for the showcase load.

What it does:

  * `scripts/production/baseline/season-2026-27.sql` — seats the eleven
    approved item types on the 2026–27 season, `on conflict (season_id, code)
    do nothing` so the file stays re-runnable; back-generates one pending
    `onboarding_items` row per (membership, type) for every membership already
    in that season, idempotent through `onboarding_items_one_per_type`; and
    adds a `do $items$` block that refuses the run if the season does not end
    up carrying exactly eleven. The verification select at the end gains
    `onboarding_item_type_count`, expected 11.

    `sort_order` is the list index, the order the inventory is written in.
    `verification_class` is `trust` only for BUCS Play and Hudl (W4's locked
    decision) and `direct` for everything else. The values are the same eleven
    Brian inserted by hand and the same eleven the application now holds.

  * `scripts/production/showcase/plan/reference.mjs` — `ONBOARDING_TYPES` is
    read from `src/lib/services/onboarding-item-types.json` rather than
    transcribed. That file is the one place the inventory is written down:
    the application reads it through
    `src/lib/services/onboarding-item-shapes.ts`, `scripts/seed-local.mjs`
    reads it, and the baseline above seats the same eleven.
    `src/lib/services/onboarding-item-shapes.test.ts` imports this module and
    fails if the two lists ever differ — before this diff is applied it
    compares the transcription, and after it, the shared file.

  * Nothing else under `scripts/production/` changes. The production preflight
    named in the issue is the baseline's own `do` block rather than
    `connection-smoke-test.mjs`: that script proves a runtime credential after
    a deploy and knows nothing about a season, and the refusal belongs in the
    file that opens the season.

Both halves were run against the local database before this diff was written:
the two inserts and the guard applied and rolled back cleanly, and the guard's
refusal was proved by pointing it at a season label that does not exist.

diff --git a/scripts/production/baseline/season-2026-27.sql b/scripts/production/baseline/season-2026-27.sql
